add('Quadratic elimination needs conjugate and zero-denominator branch checks','Quadratic elimination','2026-10-09','A','Elimination theory',
'When does eliminating T from T²=D and A(x)+TB(x)=0 preserve the desired solution branch?',
'The source describes norm elimination, reconstruction and a B=0 branch. The exact theorem clarifies why an eliminated polynomial gives necessary candidates rather than an automatic bijection. It also explains how a large resultant can contain both conjugate sheets and repeated exceptional factors.',
['Let K be a characteristic-zero field, D∈K, and A,B∈K[x]. Work first with the algebra K[T]/(T²−D).','The norm polynomial is N(x)=A(x)²−DB(x)², the determinant of multiplication by A+TB on the basis 1,T.'],
'Elimination and reconstruction with all branches',
'Every solution of T²=D and A+TB=0 satisfies N=0. If B(x)≠0, the converse holds with the unique reconstruction T=−A(x)/B(x). If B(x)=0, a solution exists exactly when A(x)=0 and T²=D; this branch must be handled separately. A prescribed real sign of T requires an additional sign test.',
'The conjugate product (A+TB)(A−TB)=A²−DB² proves necessity. For B≠0, N=0 gives (−A/B)²=D, and substitution gives A+(−A/B)B=0, proving the converse. For B=0 the original relation reduces to A=0, which cannot be inferred by dividing by B. If one starts with the fixed real constant T=√D rather than a free algebraic variable, N=0 combines both signs of √D. Reconstruction decides which sign a candidate belongs to. Over a quotient by another polynomial p, gcd(p,B) separates precisely the potentially singular components when p is squarefree. If p has nilpotents, pointwise branches alone do not capture the quotient algebra.',
'Use D=2, A=(x−1)(x−2), B=x−1.',
'The eliminant is (x−1)²[(x−2)²−2]. For T=√2, the original equation is (x−1)(x−2+√2)=0, so its roots are x=1 and x=2−√2. The eliminant also contains x=2+√2, which reconstructs T=−√2 and must be rejected on the positive sheet. At x=1, A=B=0, so both signs of T satisfy the equation although the rational reconstruction is undefined.',
'The example exhibits both a genuine exceptional branch and a conjugate extraneous candidate.',
'The recovered 26-term and 100-term counts cannot be replayed without the actual polynomials. Squaring or taking a norm can enlarge a prescribed real solution set, and integer reconstruction further requires exact divisibility and integrality tests.',
'Attach to every eliminant root an exact algebraic sign or isolating interval for −A/B; split gcd components first and record reconstruction identities in the quotient ring, producing a reversible elimination certificate.',
[('Stacks Project: finite locally free norms','https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0BD2')])
add('Squarefree quotient algebras split exceptional and regular branches by gcd','Polynomial quotient branch','2026-10-06','A','Exact algebraic branches',
'How can a denominator be inverted exactly on the valid components of a polynomial quotient without losing its exceptional components?',
'The recovered gcd branch splitting has a clean Chinese-remainder proof. It turns an informal instruction to avoid zero denominators into an exact decomposition of a finite algebra. The squarefree hypothesis is the structural reason the branches separate.',
['Let K be a field, p∈K[t] squarefree, and a∈K[t]. Set g=gcd(p,a), h=p/g, with monic choices.','Write R=K[t]/(p). A component is regular for a when multiplication by a is invertible on it.'],
'Exact regular/exceptional decomposition',
'g and h are coprime, and R≅K[t]/(g)×K[t]/(h). The image of a is zero in the first factor and invertible in the second; a Bézout identity supplies its inverse there.',
'Because p is squarefree, no irreducible factor dividing g can also divide h. The Chinese remainder map therefore has inverse constructed from Bézout polynomials ug+vh=1, proving the algebra isomorphism. Every factor of g divides a, so a is zero modulo g. Conversely, any common divisor of a and h would divide p and a, hence g, contradicting coprimality of g and h. Thus gcd(a,h)=1, and extended Euclid gives ra+sh=1; r is an exact inverse of a modulo h. No root approximations or heuristic branch labels enter. When g=1 there is no exceptional component; when h=1 every component is exceptional. These cases are naturally interpreted using the zero quotient factor.',
'Take K=Q, p=(t−1)(t+1)(t−2) and a=t−1.',
'Here g=t−1 and h=t²−t−2. In K[t]/(h), (t−1)(t/2)=(t²−t)/2=1, so t/2 is the regular inverse. In the factor t=1, a is zero. Hence an expression b/a can be evaluated using bt/2 only on the h component; the t=1 component still requires its original undivided equation. For contrast, if p=(t−1)², then g=t−1 and h=t−1 are not coprime: the quotient contains a nonzero nilpotent and cannot be replaced by a product of two point factors.',
'The decomposition makes every denominator decision and exceptional branch auditable.',
'This is an algebra decomposition, not a guarantee that all real roots are physically or arithmetically admissible. Repeated factors require primary or local-algebra methods, and real sign conditions remain additional data.',
'Combine this split with Sturm isolating intervals and norm reconstruction, so each real component carries both an exact inverse certificate and a proof of its sign and multiplicity.',
[('Stacks Project: Chinese remainder theorem','https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00DT')])
add('The wall fluctuation operator has exactly two bound-state levels','wall operator','2026-10-06','A','Spectral differential equations',
'Can the recovered levels 0 and 3k² be derived exactly, with the continuum threshold and positivity justified?',
'The explicit wall operator is a tractable Pöschl–Teller problem. Factorization proves stability and identifies its discrete modes. An intertwining argument excludes any additional bound state; the mathematical result does not certify the source’s physical mass or lifetime estimates.',
['Let k>0 and L=k²[−d²/dx²+4−6 sech²x] on L²(R), with domain H²(R).','Use the L² adjoint of d/dx, namely −d/dx, and write A=d/dx+2tanh x and B=d/dx+tanh x.'],
'Exact discrete and essential spectrum',
'L/k²=A* A≥0. Its discrete eigenvalues are 0 and 3, each simple, with eigenfunctions sech²x and sech x·tanh x respectively; its essential spectrum is [4,∞).',
'Using (tanh x)′=sech²x and tanh²x=1−sech²x gives A*A=−d²+4−6sech²x. The kernel solves Af=0, hence f=C sech²x, which is square-integrable and unique. The partner AA*=−d²+4−2sech²x equals B*B+3, while BB*=−d²+1. The kernel of B is span(sech x), giving an eigenvalue three of AA*. Applying A* yields a nonzero multiple of sech x·tanh x, hence the second eigenfunction of A*A. If A*A had any other positive eigenvalue λ<4, A would transport its eigenfunction to an eigenfunction of B*B with eigenvalue λ−3. Positivity excludes λ<3. For 3<λ<4, applying B would produce an L² eigenfunction of −d²+1 below one, impossible by its Fourier transform. At λ=3 the kernel of B is one-dimensional. The decaying bounded potential is a relatively compact perturbation of −d²+4, so the essential spectrum remains [4,∞); equivalently, the standard resolvent compactness follows by local Rellich compactness and a small sup-norm tail.',
'Check k=2 and normalize the zero mode.',
'The levels become 0 and 12, with continuum beginning at 16. Since ∫R sech⁴x dx=4/3, the normalized zero mode is (√3/2)sech²x; substitution t=tanh x reduces that integral to ∫_(−1)^1(1−t²)dt.',
'The spectrum and nonnegativity are exact consequences of the stated operator.',
'Coupled-field fluctuation matrices, resonance widths and unit conversions require their actual operators and parameters. A translational zero mode does not imply every coupled model is stable.',
'For a specified coupled operator, use a matrix factorization or an explicit coercive lower bound on the orthogonal complement of its symmetry modes; state the domain and threshold channels before inferring stability.',
[('Lekner: reflectionless eigenstates of the sech² potential','https://doi.org/10.1119/1.2787015')])
add('Darboux cofactors give a certificate for rational first integrals','Darboux polynomials','2026-10-09','A','Polynomial dynamical systems',
'When do exact cofactor identities combine into a conserved quantity, and what are the domain restrictions?',
'The source reports six invariant factors checked by their cofactors, but does not supply the six polynomials. A general cofactor theorem and a nontrivial explicit vector field provide a reproducible constructive replacement without pretending to replay those missing checks.',
['A polynomial vector field X acts as a derivation on polynomials. A nonzero polynomial H is Darboux with cofactor K when X(H)=KH.','A rational first integral I satisfies X(I)=0 wherever its denominator is nonzero.'],
'Cofactor cancellation and invariant hypersurfaces',
'If X(H_j)=K_jH_j and integers m_j satisfy Σm_jK_j=0, then I=∏H_j^(m_j) is a rational first integral. Each zero hypersurface H_j=0 is invariant under the local flow.',
'The derivation product and quotient rules give X(I)=IΣm_jX(H_j)/H_j=IΣm_jK_j on the open set where all negative-power factors are nonzero; cancellation proves the rational identity. Along a solution trajectory, H_j obeys the scalar linear equation dH_j/dt=K_j(x(t))H_j. Its unique solution is the initial value times an exponential integral, so an initial zero remains zero while the trajectory exists. Thus the hypersurface statement follows without dividing by H_j. Polynomial identities X(H_j)−K_jH_j=0 can be checked coefficient by coefficient, providing exact finite certificates. Cofactor dependence is sufficient for an integral, but a collection of invariant factors need not have any nontrivial integer relation among its cofactors.',
'Choose X=(x²−y²)∂_x+2xy∂_y.',
'For H₁=y, X(H₁)=2xy=2xH₁. For H₂=x²+y², X(H₂)=2x(x²−y²)+4xy²=2xH₂. The cofactors agree, so I=(x²+y²)/y is constant on trajectories with y≠0. Direct differentiation gives numerator y·2x(x²+y²)−(x²+y²)·2xy=0. The excluded line y=0 is itself invariant and carries x′=x², so its trajectories require a separate chart.',
'The same algebra proves conservation and records exactly where the rational formula is defined.',
'No completeness of all first integrals follows from finding several factors. Constants of motion do not guarantee global existence, and the missing historical six-factor calculation is not claimed verified.',
'Search cofactors in a bounded-degree polynomial space, solve exact linear dependence relations, and use the resulting integrals to reduce a specified flow on regular level sets while analyzing singular divisors separately.',
[('Darboux polynomials and integrability: original research archive','https://arxiv.org/abs/1009.2876')])
add('Schröder jets are solved degree by degree until a resonance occurs','jets/Schröder','2026-10-14','T','Formal dynamical systems',
'What exact hypotheses make a finite-jet linearization unique, and why can early jets fail to identify later coefficients?',
'The dated synthesis lists jets and Schröder equations, while another passage warns that a seventh-order coefficient leaves six lower orders unchanged. A formal coefficient argument explains both phenomena. The chosen example is explicitly solvable and does not assume convergence of a general formal conjugacy.',
['Let f(x)=λx+Σ_(n≥2)f_nxⁿ over a characteristic-zero field, λ≠0. Seek h(x)=x+Σ_(n≥2)h_nxⁿ with h(f(x))=λh(x).','The N-jet is a power series modulo x^(N+1). A resonance at degree n means λⁿ=λ.'],
'Nonresonant jet recursion',
'If λⁿ≠λ for 2≤n≤N, the normalized Schröder equation has exactly one N-jet solution. Each h_n depends only on f₂,…,f_n; changing f_(N+1) cannot alter the solution through degree N.',
'Assume h₂,…,h_(n−1) have been determined. In degree n, the contribution of h_nf(x)ⁿ is λⁿh_n; the right side contributes λh_n. Every remaining term uses only lower coefficients of h and coefficients of f through degree n. Thus (λⁿ−λ)h_n is a known scalar, uniquely determining h_n in the nonresonant case. Induction proves existence and uniqueness to every requested finite order and the triangular dependency. At a resonance the known scalar must vanish for solvability; if it vanishes, h_n is not determined at that order. This proves a precise obstruction rather than a general linearization promise. In particular, two maps differing first at degree seven have identical six-jets, so no procedure using only those six coefficients can distinguish them.',
'Take f(x)=2x+x².',
'Here 1+f(x)=(1+x)², so h(x)=log(1+x) gives h(f(x))=2h(x) as a formal identity. Its first coefficients are x−x²/2+x³/3−x⁴/4. Direct degree-two comparison gives 1+4h₂=2h₂, hence h₂=−1/2; degree three gives 4h₂+8h₃=2h₃, hence h₃=1/3. For contrast, λ=−1 resonates at every odd degree greater than one.',
'The example links an exact conjugacy to the finite triangular calculation and makes the identifiability limit explicit.',
'Formal existence is not analytic convergence; small denominators require additional estimates. The source’s specific coefficient 1/128 cannot be independently checked without its actual input map.',
'For a supplied analytic family, bound the coefficient recursion on a stated disk and derive a certified remainder after N terms; separately classify resonant obstructions instead of hiding them in an inverse matrix.',
[('NIST DLMF: logarithmic power series','https://dlmf.nist.gov/4.6')])
